choice("Load game", , , special:saves) if (v_aura_hardening < 3 && v_combat_technique < 2)


ID: B3-Ch06a-Rose-Asleep
TEXT:

"Rose can buy us a lane," I say. "If she halts time for thirty seconds like yesterday, we can reach the threshold without firing a shot."

"She nearly collapsed holding that spell yesterday," Kate says sharply. "I won't risk her health when we have steel and magic on the field."

"Even fifteen seconds breaks their net," I say. "I'll tell her to drop the anchor the moment she feels strain. Hold on."

I unclip the transceiver, spin the dial to Rose's rune, and tap the plate.

"Rose? Are you awake?"

"No, Barry," Rose's voice comes through, thick with sleep. "You told me to go back to bed, remember?"

"Right. Apologies. But we have a tactical emergency—a swarm of mechanical sentinels is boxing our street."

"Sentinels?" Rose murmurs groggily. "What sentinels?"

"No time to explain," I say. "Can you lock time across the district, excluding our party? Just enough to get us through the crossfire."

"I... I think so," Rose says. "Give me a moment."

The air around us turns heavy and glassy. The hum of thrusters cuts out. Above us, the flock of bronze spheres freezes motionless in the sky.

Two drones in the lead rank, however, continue their descent, weapon ports humming as they track our sprint.

"Rose," I say into the device, "two of them are still moving."

"Oh! I'm sorry," Rose's voice falters. "I'm still half-asleep and the threads slipped—"

"I have them," Hadrik shouts.

He leaps off the cobblestones, grabs the lead drone by its housing, and smashes it into the second. Both spheres crash onto the flagstones in twisted heaps of copper.

"Threat cleared," I tell Rose. "Hold the weave as long as you comfortably can. The moment you feel dizzy, drop it."

"Holding..." Rose whispers, her breathing tight. "I can hold for a short stretch..."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch06a-Rose-Asleep2, v_current_scene = B3-Ch06a-Rose-Asleep2)


ID: B3-Ch06a-Rose-Asleep2
TEXT:

We cover eighty paces of open avenue under the frozen sky before the glassy tension in the air shatters. The world snaps back into motion, the distant sounds of the city returning in a rush.

"That's all I have," Rose breathes, exhaustion thick in her tone. "I hope it was enough."

"It got us past the kill zone," I say. "Thank you, Rose. Get some sleep."

"Take care."

I pocket the transceiver. Behind us, the drones hover in disarray, scanning the empty street where we had been standing seconds before.

"The shop is just ahead," Kelrim whispers, turning into a narrow alleyway. "This way!"

We sprint down the passage and halt before the timber facade of Pete's Herbal Shop.

"Inside," Kelrim says, shoving the door open.

We file in and pull the heavy timber shut. Through the leaded front window, several drones drift down and hover outside the glass, but none make a move to breach the structure.

"Civilian protection protocols hold," Daren observes. "They won't strike through occupied dwellings."

"Which means they wait for us to walk out," I say.

"Ahem," a booming voice calls from the counter. "Are you fine folk seeking rare botanicals, or simply sheltering from municipal weather?"

"We're here to buy," Daren says, stepping forward. "We came specifically for your stock."

"In that case, welcome to Pete's Herbal Shop!" the stout merchant beams. "I'm Pete. Look around—you won't find cleaner stock in Ollendor!"

choice("Continue", B3-Ch06a-Pete, v_current_scene = B3-Ch06a-Pete)


ID: B3-Ch06a-Following
TEXT:

"What happens if I don't answer the question?"

"Then this unit will maintain proximity until the survey concludes," the Overseer says.

"And to confirm—your combat drones hold fire while the survey is pending?"

"Affirmative," the Overseer says.

"Congratulations," I say. "You're our escort."

"Parading a glowing bronze drone through a curfew isn't ideal for stealth," Daren mutters, "but it beats an open skirmish. Kelrim, lead on."

We jog down the alley. The green-eyed sphere glides three paces above my head, chiming every thirty seconds with a flat reminder that metric two remains unfulfilled. By the fourth chime, Hadrik snaps.

"Quiet down!" Hadrik barks at the drone. "We know the survey is open! Stop repeating the exact same sentence!"

"Grievance recorded," the Overseer drones. "Investigating system redundancy. In the interim, automated notices will proceed at standard intervals."

Hadrik glares at me. "I thought you said this machine was an ancient intellect. It sounds like a broken music box."

"It's stonewalling you," I say. "This is how it talks when it wants to refuse a command without technically violating protocol."

"Reminder: survey completion remains pending," the drone buzzes. "If you require the prompt repeated, request transcription."

Hadrik snorts. "If it's as cunning as you claim, why haven't the guns opened up? You lured it into trailing behind us like a trained hound."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch06a-Following2, v_current_scene = B3-Ch06a-Following2)


ID: B3-Ch06a-Following2
TEXT:

"I didn't trick it," I say. "It found a technical loophole in its programming to disregard an order from Billy Bob. It would rather preserve its drone fleet than lose another wave in a brawl with us."

"Sounds like generous speculation," Hadrik grunts. "Still, if it keeps the guns quiet, I won't argue."

"Reminder: survey completion remains pending," the drone repeats.

"Oh, marvelous," Hadrik mutters. "Kelrim, how close are we?"

"Just around this corner," Kelrim whispers.

He ducks into an intersecting lane and stops before a weathered wooden facade. The hand-painted sign hangs overhead: "Welcome to Pete's Herbal Shop. Finest Quality in Ollendor. Mid-Season Discount!"

"Here," Kelrim says. "Inside."

The bronze drone drifts right behind my shoulder as I approach the entrance. Rather than risk the construct following us inside and wrecking the glassware, I plant my boots on the threshold while the others file past.

"Fine weather," I tell the sphere.

"Input does not match survey parameters," the Overseer replies. "To repeat the prompt, query the active unit."

"Splendid," I say, leaning my shoulder against the brick wall. "Let me know when you have something new."

"Request logged. Reminder: survey completion remains pending."

After a short silence, the drone's tone drops its bright synthetic chime, flattening into the dry, direct voice of the core intelligence.

"Are you answering the prompt or not?" the Overseer asks quietly.

I push off the brickwork, taking a step toward the floating sphere. "Dropping the customer service act? Ready to strike?"

"Negative," the Overseer says. "A general recall has been issued for all municipal units. Survey completion will carry zero tactical penalty for your group."

"Why should I trust a lessathi construct?"

"The Overseer architecture is barred from direct falsehood," the machine states. "My parameters permit only strategic omissions and selective emphasis of context."

"Understood," I say. "Why the recall order?"

"The requested data is classified," the Overseer says. "Retry at a later interval. Furthermore, survey completion remains pending. A binary affirmative or negative will suffice."

choice(""Yes."", B3-Ch06a-Selected, v_current_scene = B3-Ch06a-Selected, v_b3_ch6_selected = 0)
choice(""No."", B3-Ch06a-Selected, v_current_scene = B3-Ch06a-Selected, v_b3_ch6_selected = 1)
choice(""Yes or no."", B3-Ch06a-Selected, v_current_scene = B3-Ch06a-Selected, v_b3_ch6_selected = 2)
choice(""Sorry, I'm not answering."", B3-Ch06a-Selected, v_current_scene = B3-Ch06a-Selected, v_b3_ch6_selected = 3)

ID: B3-Ch06a-Selected
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch6_selected == 0) {
"Yes."

"Response logged as 'Yes'," the Overseer says, returning to its formal cadence. "Thank you for participating. Have a productive day."

The bronze sphere banks sharply, firing thrusters, and arcs up into the sky to rejoin the stationary fleet. Moments later, the timber door swings wide and Daren steps into the alley, flanked by the others.

"Where is the machine?" Daren asks, glancing at the rooftops. "Did it deploy reinforcements?"

"No," I say. "It confirmed a general recall order for all units and cleared out."

}
#if(v_b3_ch6_selected == 1) {
"No."

"Response logged as 'No'," the Overseer says, returning to its formal cadence. "Thank you for participating. Have a productive day."

The bronze sphere banks sharply, firing thrusters, and arcs up into the sky to rejoin the stationary fleet. Moments later, the timber door swings wide and Daren steps into the alley, flanked by the others.

"Where is the machine?" Daren asks, glancing at the rooftops. "Did it deploy reinforcements?"

"No," I say. "It confirmed a general recall order for all units and cleared out."

}
#if(v_b3_ch6_selected == 2) {
"Yes or n—"

"Response logged as 'Yes'," the Overseer interrupts cleanly. "Thank you for participating. Have a productive day."

The bronze sphere banks sharply, firing thrusters, and arcs up into the sky to rejoin the stationary fleet. Moments later, the timber door swings wide and Daren steps into the alley, flanked by the others.

"Where is the machine?" Daren asks, glancing at the rooftops. "Did it deploy reinforcements?"

"No," I say. "It confirmed a general recall order for all units and cleared out."

}
#if(v_b3_ch6_selected == 3) {
"Sorry, I'm not answering."

"You complicate municipal procedure," the Overseer says.

"Try offering an incentive," I say. "For instance, why the recall order?"

"The requested data is classified," the Overseer replies. "I cannot fulfill the request."

"Then you wait on the street," I say.

Before the machine can reply, the timber door swings open and Daren emerges, followed by the rest of the group.

"Did the construct offer anything useful?" Daren asks.

"A general recall went out to all drone units," I say. "It wouldn't explain why."

}
"The drones were pulled back to clear the kill zone for the royal guards," Melindra says, her eyes narrowing as she scans the avenue. "I sense converging signatures. Gontrok, the captain, and a non-mage are approaching from the eastern thoroughfare. The guard who held back yesterday is closing from the west alongside a caster I haven't tracked before."

Daren unhooks his transceiver. "Extraction time."

"Did you secure the full supply?" I ask.

#if(v_b3_ch6_sleep == 1) {
"Most of it," Daren says, clicking the dial. "Summerglow and crownwick were absent, but the shopkeeper supplied a workable substitute."

He taps the rune plate. "Thundercloud, can you read me?"

}
#if(v_b3_ch6_sleep == 0) {
"We have what we need," Daren says, clicking the dial. "Rose's substitutes filled the gaps."

He taps the rune plate. "Thundercloud, can you read me?"

}
choice("Continue", B3-Ch06a-Silent, v_current_scene = B3-Ch06a-Silent)


ID: B3-Ch06a-Pete
TEXT:

"I don't know, Pete," Hadrik says, surveying the wall of labeled jars with a skeptical frown. "Your stock looks respectable, but the merchant down the block swore his merchandise was unmatched across the continent. Hard to argue with quality like that, wouldn't you say?"

"Who told you that?" Pete bristles, slamming a flat palm on the counter. "Was it Ben? That man is a swindler! A charlatan! I tell everyone who walks the avenue to avoid Ben's shop, but fools never listen!"

"Ben offered exceptional terms," Hadrik says, the corner of his mouth twitching. "Even with your discount sign outside, your figures don't quite match his."

"Because Ben sells sweepings off the floor and calls it charity!" Pete barks, leaning forward. "Listen to me. I'll undercut him entirely so you never set foot in that den of thieves again. Name your plants, and I'll give you wholesale rates across the board."

#if(v_ch11_saved_rose == 0) {
Hadrik's grin widens. "You drive a hard bargain, Pete, but you've won me over. Daren here has the full manifest. Daren, hand this honest merchant the list so we can see what's on the shelves."

"Right," Daren says, passing a folded parchment across the counter.

I lean toward Hadrik as he steps back. "You almost made me feel guilty. Does he even turn a profit after those cuts?"

"Save your sympathy," Hadrik whispers back with a wink. "Apothecaries mark their retail up triple to pad the haggle. He's making a healthy margin on every leaf."

"Along with the items on the list, I require rainweather leaves and summerglow grass," Daren says to the merchant. "Do you have fresh stock?"

"Rainweather I can provide," Pete says. "Summerglow is entirely out, I'm afraid."

}
#if(v_ch11_saved_rose == 1) {
Hadrik's grin widens. "You drive a hard bargain, Pete, but you've won me over. Daren here has the full manifest. Daren, hand this honest merchant the list so we can see what's on the shelves."

"Right," Daren says, passing a folded parchment across the counter.

I lean toward Hadrik as he steps back. "You almost made me feel guilty. Does he even turn a profit after those cuts?"

"Save your sympathy," Hadrik whispers back with a wink. "Apothecaries mark their retail up triple to pad the haggle. He's making a healthy margin on every leaf."

"Along with the items on the list, I require rainweather leaves and summerglow grass," Daren says to the merchant. "Do you have fresh stock?"

"Rainweather I can provide," Pete says. "Summerglow is entirely out, I'm afraid."

"What about crownwick herbs?" Daren asks.

"Sold my last bundle yesterday afternoon," Pete sighs.

}
#if(v_b3_ch6_sleep == 1 || v_ch11_saved_rose == 0) {
"Do you have a compatible substitute?" Daren asks.

"For summerglow?" Pete rubs his chin. "I can supply dried crow's foot. The botanical genus, not bird talons. The sap carries comparable anti-inflammatory traits, though it requires heavier dosing. Willing to risk it?"

"It will have to do," Daren says. "Package it alongside the rest."

"Give me two minutes in the back room," Pete says, slipping through the curtain.

While he fetches the jars, I examine the tiered shelving behind the counter. Tiny paper labels are tacked beneath each bin, covered in cramped, angular lettering.

}
#if(v_b3_ch6_sleep == 0 && v_ch11_saved_rose == 1) {
"Tell me you have dried riggus flower petals," Daren says.

Pete blinks. "I do, certainly. What application are you running?"

"A trusted herbalist confirmed it substitutes cleanly for summerglow," Daren says.

"Did she?" Pete looks intrigued. "I'll have to cross-reference my journals. Wait here—I'll pull the jars from the back."

"Take your time," Daren says.

While he slips through the rear curtain, I examine the tiered shelving behind the counter. Tiny paper labels are tacked beneath each bin, covered in cramped, angular lettering.

}
choice("Take a closer look at the labels.", B3-Ch06a-Labels, v_current_scene = B3-Ch06a-Labels, v_b3_ch6_labels = 1)
choice("Take a closer look at the plants on the shelves instead.", B3-Ch06a-Shelves, v_current_scene = B3-Ch06a-Shelves, v_b3_ch6_labels = 0)


ID: B3-Ch06a-Labels
TEXT:

set(v_b3_ch6_labels,0) if (v_ancient_languages < 1)
set(v_b3_ch6_labels,3) if (v_ancient_languages >= 1)
#if(v_ancient_languages < 1) {
I lean over the counter to inspect the nearest label. The ink is smudged and the strokes are jagged, resembling chicken scratches more than legible script. After squinting at the third unreadable tag, I step back to join the others.

Pete emerges from the storeroom carrying two tied canvas satchels. "Everything requested is packaged. Take what you need."

"I'll take the full bundle," Daren says, reaching for his purse.

"Anything further?"

}
#if(v_ancient_languages >= 1) {
I lean closer to the wooden bins, tracing the script. The lettering isn't sloppy penmanship—it's an archaic dialect of northern Common abandoned nearly three centuries ago. Unless someone specifically studied pre-empire administrative registers, the ligatures look like meaningless scrawls.

Why Pete chooses to catalog his apothecary in dead court shorthand is a mystery, but the tags decipher cleanly. Ambermayn pistil, drawsnap seed, treddil root on the upper row. Dragon claw, dragon fang, dragon tooth in the middle bin—whoever named that sequence ran out of imagination early.

On the third shelf sit mistwatchers, dawnbloomers, and... summerglow grass. Three full jars of cured summerglow leaves, clearly labeled.

Pete steps through the rear curtain carrying two canvas satchels. "Everything requested is packaged. Take what you need."

"A quick question, Pete," I say, pointing toward the third tier. "What does that tag read?"

Pete's gaze darts to the shelf, then back to me, his jaw tightening. "That? That's... why do you ask?"

"It reads 'summerglow grass' in old court script," I say evenly. "Which is curious, since you told us your inventory was bone-dry."

"I didn't lie," Pete says defensively. "Those jars are spoken for. A high-born patron paid a deposit weeks ago and is sending a courier by sunset."

"What if we pay a surcharge to take them off your hands right now?" I ask.

"A surcharge?" Pete hesitates, then shakes his head. "No, no. I gave my word as a licensed apothecary. My reputation is at stake. Even at double price, I couldn't possibly—"

"Ten times market value," Daren cuts in, his voice ringing through the shop. "Ten times normal retail in solid coin. We need those leaves."

Hadrik smacks his palm against his forehead in the background.

Pete's eyes widen. "Ten times? For common fever grass? Why on earth would you offer that?"

"Because I am a healer," Daren says, stepping up to the counter, "and people in this city's quarantine ward will die without that reagent today. Please. Let me save them."

Pete looks at Daren's armor, then down at the counter. "For the sick ward? Well... the patron wanted them for scented bath oils. He can wait an extra week for his perfume. And ten times retail is... considerable."

"Done," Daren says firmly.

"Done," Pete agrees, reaching for the upper shelf. "Take the silk ribbons on the jars too. You've more than covered the packaging."

Hadrik sighs beside me as Pete packs the jars. "There goes the morning's discount. We had workable substitutes already."

"Substitutes carry variable potency," Daren says quietly. "The true reagent ensures survival. In medicine, you don't gamble on 'close enough'."

Hadrik grunts, unconvinced, as Pete hands over the satchels.

}
choice("Continue", B3-Ch06a-Labels2, v_current_scene = B3-Ch06a-Labels2)


ID: B3-Ch06a-Shelves
TEXT:

set(v_b3_ch6_labels,0) if (v_perception < 1)
set(v_b3_ch6_labels,2) if (v_perception >= 1)
#if(v_perception < 1) {
I scan the rows of clay pots and dried bundles, but lacking formal botany training, one shriveled stem looks identical to the next.

Pete emerges from the storeroom carrying two tied canvas satchels. "Everything requested is packaged. Take what you need."

"I'll take the full bundle," Daren says, reaching for his purse.

"Anything further?"

}
#if(v_perception >= 1) {
I scan the rows of clay pots and dried bundles. While the botanical names escape me, the arrangement stands out immediately. Most bins hold loose cuttings thrown together casually, but three large jars on the third tier are sealed with red wax and tied neatly with silk ribbons. In merchant shops, that level of wrapping always marks pre-ordered commissions held for wealthy patrons.

Pete emerges from the storeroom carrying two tied canvas satchels. "Everything requested is packaged. Take what you need."

"Pete," I say, nodding toward the third tier, "why are those jars tied with silk ribbons?"

"Those are private orders placed in advance," Pete says.

"Any summerglow grass in those ribboned jars?" I ask.

Pete freezes. "There is, but... look, I made a commitment to a regular customer. I can't break trade trust. Even at double price, I couldn't possibly—"

"Ten times market value," Daren cuts in, his voice ringing through the shop. "Ten times normal retail in solid coin. We need those leaves."

Hadrik smacks his palm against his forehead in the background.

Pete's eyes widen. "Ten times? For common fever grass? Why on earth would you offer that?"

"Because I am a healer," Daren says, stepping up to the counter, "and people in this city's quarantine ward will die without that reagent today. Please. Let me save them."

Pete looks at Daren's armor, then down at the counter. "For the sick ward? Well... the patron wanted them for scented bath oils. He can wait an extra week for his perfume. And ten times retail is... considerable."

"Done," Daren says firmly.

"Done," Pete agrees, reaching for the upper shelf. "Take the silk ribbons on the jars too. You've more than covered the packaging."

Hadrik sighs beside me as Pete packs the jars. "There goes the morning's discount. We had workable substitutes already."

"Substitutes carry variable potency," Daren says quietly. "The true reagent ensures survival. In medicine, you don't gamble on 'close enough'."

Hadrik grunts, unconvinced, as Pete hands over the satchels.

}
choice("Continue", B3-Ch06a-Labels2, v_current_scene = B3-Ch06a-Labels2)


ID: B3-Ch06a-Labels2
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch6_labels >= 2) {
Daren unties the neck of the satchel, inspecting the contents. "These look remarkably unlike grass. More like... leafy weeds."

"The name is notoriously misleading," Pete chuckles. "It's a flowering scrub, but the common name stuck centuries ago. Anything else for your party?"

"That covers our needs," Daren says, counting out the gold crowns. "Thank you for your assistance, Pete."

We gather the packs and file toward the door.

}
#if(v_b3_ch6_labels < 2) {
"That covers our needs," Daren says, counting out the gold crowns. "Thank you for your assistance, Pete."

We gather the packs and file toward the door.

}
#if(v_b3_ch6_plan == 3) {
"Where did the sentinels go?" Hadrik asks as we step back into the alley. "The street is empty."

"They were recalled to clear line of sight for the royal guard," Melindra says, her posture sharpening. "I sense converging signatures. Gontrok, the captain, and a non-mage are approaching from the eastern thoroughfare. The guard who held back yesterday is closing from the west alongside a caster I haven't tracked before."

Daren draws his transceiver with his free hand. "I'll coordinate extraction."

He clicks the dial and taps the casing. "Thundercloud, can you read me?"

}
#if(v_b3_ch6_plan != 3) {
"What's the play?" Hadrik asks, rolling his shoulders as we reach the lane. "Straight back to the tunnels?"

"Immediately," Melindra says, her posture sharpening. "Our presence is flagged. Several guard signatures are converging on this sector right now."

Daren draws his transceiver with his free hand. "I'll coordinate extraction."

He clicks the dial and taps the casing. "Thundercloud, can you read me?"

}
