ID: B3-Ch06a-Confession
TEXT:

"Listen, everyone," I say, clearing my throat. "This is slightly sudden, but I have a small admission."

"Not the moment, Barry," Daren mutters, checking his sword belt.

"Bear with me for ten seconds," I say. "During the God of Time's trial, I realized that my self-preservation instincts are somewhat defective."

Hadrik stops stretching and blinks at me. "You only realized that now?"

"The memory made it undeniable," I say. "I'm trying to exercise better judgment, but old habits run deep. If you hear me proposing an idiotic scheme in the heat of battle, smack me across the back of the head. It helps reset my thinking."

Hadrik steps up, clapping a heavy hand on my shoulder. "Barry, you can rely on me to club you across the skull whenever requested. Not for personal amusement, mind you—purely as a favor to a comrade."

"Excellent," I say. "On that note, I have a tactical thought for the opening exchange. If Melindra casts updrafts on me, I can fly ahead as a mobile decoy, drawing their vanguard with crossbow fire while the rest of you surround the stragglers and—"

A sharp crack of frost explodes against the back of my skull.

Pain shoots down my neck. I stumble forward, rubbing the stinging welt, and spin around.

Kate stands three paces away, balancing a six-inch icicle between her fingers, her dark eyes narrowing in disgust.

"I knew you couldn't breathe for thirty seconds without volunteering for a suicide run," Kate says coldly. "We settled this yesterday. We do not split the formation. We fight the guard together. Understood?"

"Understood," I mutter, rubbing the sore spot behind my ear.

The icicle in Kate's grip dissolves into fine mist. "Gontrok's field is flaring. He's close."

"He broke into a sprint," Melindra confirms, pointing down the northern avenue. "He hits our line first. Brace yourself, Hadrik."

Kelrim looks between us, his ears drooping as he grips his staff. "One clarification before the melee begins... When you spoke earlier of slaying a god... that was rhetorical exaggeration, correct?"

We stare at the goblin in silence.

Kelrim's voice climbs an octave. "It was exaggeration, wasn't it?"

A shrieking cackle erupts from the golden amulet at Flower's chest. "Rhetorical! The goblin thinks it was a metaphor! Keep him around as a pet—his face is magnificent! Ahahaha!"

choice("Continue", B3-Ch06b-Rematch, v_current_scene = B3-Ch06b-Rematch)
